00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.860
Doctor Strange.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.210
Doctor Strange originally had an introductory
prologue set at CERN, the European nuclear

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:09.340
research facility that carries out research
into particle physics and which is also home

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.409
to the Large Hadron Collider.

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:15.340
Marvel Studios head honcho Kevin Feige has
said that the organization's research into

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.009
high-energy physics and its investigation
of the sub-atomic world would have been a

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:23.909
great real world setting and way of introducing
fictional alternate dimensions into the Marvel

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:25.310
Cinematic Universe.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.380
Although the CERN prologue was scrapped, Feige
has said it's possible that it might be used

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.819
in a Doctor Strange sequel.

00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:37.099
Tina Minoru, played by actress Linda Louise
Duan, has a tiny cameo in the movie, but promotional

00:01:37.099 --> 00:01:41.820
TV spots show a brief scene with her wielding
the iconic Staff of One, which is a powerful

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.329
magical object in the comics that Tina's daughter
Nico acquires and later wields.

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:50.959
Speaking of which, Nico Minoru will be a lead
character in Marvel's TV adaptation of The

00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:52.419
Runaways on Hulu.

00:01:52.419 --> 00:01:56.639
Unfortunately, the scene with Tina was cut
from the movie's theatrical release, though

00:01:56.639 --> 00:01:59.929
it seems likely that it would have been part
of the sequence where Wong and his crew are

00:01:59.929 --> 00:02:03.469
preparing to battle Kaecilius at the Hong
Kong Sanctum Sanctorum.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:07.749
Tina's character does appear a little more
in Marvel's official Doctor Strange prelude

00:02:07.749 --> 00:02:11.819
comic where she works with Daniel Drumm and
other masters from Kamar-Taj to recover a

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:15.209
magical item called the Dark Sceptre from
a thief.

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:19.680
And it really seems that there was also an
extended scene at the Hong Kong Sanctum Sanctorum

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.209
that was cut from the film too.

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:26.040
For example, in the film, we do see Wong silently
grabbing the Wand of Watoomb as he's readying

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:31.129
for Kaecilius's attack, but the official B-roll
for the movie has more footage of Wong brandishing

00:02:31.129 --> 00:02:33.400
the wand and also talking to the camera.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.079
In the theatrical release, we only see the
aftermath of the attack by Kaecilius on Hong

00:02:38.079 --> 00:02:42.409
Kong, so this deleted scene with Wong might
have shown a confrontation between Wong and

00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:45.849
the Zealots and could filled in the gap in
the story before Wong's death.

00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:50.019
There was originally going to be an easter
egg to the Ebony Blade in the movie but, in

00:02:50.019 --> 00:02:51.440
the end, it was removed.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.880
The Ebony Blade is an enchanted sword that
appeared in the first issue of Marvel's Black

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:56.880
Knight comics.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.730
In the movie, the mystical blade was going
to be included in a magic book called The

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:04.719
Lexicon of Relics, but it "was taken out of
the script" according to the head of the art

00:03:04.719 --> 00:03:06.219
department Barry Gibbs.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:10.310
Interestingly, there is a little easter egg
to Black Knight's helmet among the relics

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.170
at the New York Sanctum.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.969
Director Scott Derrickson has confirmed that
there was a whole scene filmed with actress

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:21.090
Lulu Wilson, star of Ouija: Origin of Evil,
who played Stephen Strange's sister, Donna.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.890
In the comics, it's Stephen's younger sister
Donna who sparks his interest in medicine

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:28.060
after she injures herself and he has to tend
to her.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.060
And more tragically, when she accidentally
drowns, Strange becomes a lot more cynical

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.330
and uncaring.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:37.420
The deleted scene with Donna would have given
more depth to Strange's backstory; however,

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:41.000
Derrickson has said that it was removed because
it ultimately didn't fit with the tone of

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.420
the film.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.700
After Rachel McAdams was announced as a female
lead in Doctor Strange, there was speculation

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.150
that she would be playing Doctor Strange's
best-known girlfriend, Clea.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:55.079
In the comics, Clea is Dormammu's niece and
originates from the Dark Dimension, however,

00:03:55.079 --> 00:03:59.029
Marvel felt that McAdams's character should
be grounded in the real world rather than

00:03:59.029 --> 00:04:04.340
other-worldly, and be somebody who could act
as an anchor for Doctor Strange in New York.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.620
The filmmakers also believed there were already
enough new concepts to establish in this first

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:14.120
film and as Kevin Feige put it "an interdimensional
girlfriend that's the daughter of an interdimensional

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.419
demonesque creature was a step too far in
introducing this world."

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:22.120
Rachel McAdams, of course, plays Christine
Palmer, who's is just one of the characters

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.740
who've taken on the mantle of Night Nurse
in the comics.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:29.020
While the film does make some subtle allusions
to her comic book history as Night Nurse,

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.490
Christine Palmer in the movie never uses the
Night Nurse alias and, in fact, she's actually

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:38.080
a doctor, although some movie critics seem
rather confused and, despite having seen the

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.430
film, still think McAdams is playing a nurse.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:45.020
Anyway, Marvel's Kevin Feige hasn't closed
down rumours that Christine Palmer may still

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:50.110
take on the alias of Night Nurse in the MCU,
saying that "it's just as much fun for us

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:53.180
as it is for the fans to speculate about that".

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.430
In the post-credits scene, when Mordo takes
away Jonathan Pangborn's magic, rendering

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:01.830
him paralysed once more, we witness Mordo's
conversion to the villainous role that his

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.090
character usually has in the comics.

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:10.639
However, concept art reportedly shown by Marvel
at D23 Expo in 2015 showed Strange in battle

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:15.169
with Mordo, suggesting that there were initially
plans to use Mordo either as the original

00:05:15.169 --> 00:05:18.500
villain or have him turn against Strange earlier
in the movie.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:23.830
A possible villain cut from the film is the
tentacled demonic monster Shuma Gorath, who

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.400
features in Marvel's comics and was teased
via a LEGO set of Doctor Strange's Sanctum

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:29.400
Sanctorum.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.080
The demon could be one of the weird and wonderful
ideas that the movie's screenwriters Cargill

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.660
and Derrickson said they originally pitched
but were asked to keep for future movies.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:43.600
Amy Landecker, star of the comedy-drama Transparent,
filmed scenes as a character called Dr Bruner,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.729
but her scenes were confirmed by Scott Derrickson
as having been cut from the final edit.

00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:51.729
Which suggests there were more scenes set
at the hospital where Strange works.

00:05:51.729 --> 00:05:56.129
And Landecker's character Dr Bruner could
also be an easter egg to artist Frank Brunner

00:05:56.129 --> 00:05:58.409
who's worked on numerous Doctor Strange comics.

